{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target constructs a false dilemma by assuming that 'pragmatic preference' and 'progress toward truth' (verisimilitude) are mutually exclusive. Popper explicitly developed the concept of verisimilitude to allow for a notion of progress (getting closer to the truth) without relying on inductive probability. The target's mechanism asserts that the pragmatic defense 'forces the concession that corroboration offers no grip on verisimilitude,' but this contradicts Popper's own stance where preferring the better-corroborated theory is precisely the rational method for pursuing higher verisimilitude, even if that preference cannot be justified inductively. By conflating the lack of inductive justification with the lack of a truth-directed goal, the target misrepresents the 'cost' as a loss of teleology, whereas Popper argues the teleology (truth) remains the regulative ideal, while the pragmatic rule is the only non-inductive way to pursue it.","counterexample":["Popper, K. R. (1963). Conjectures and Refutations: The Growth of Scientific Knowledge. (Specifically the discussion on Verisimilitude in Chapter 10, where he argues we prefer theories with higher content and corroboration because they are our best candidates for being closer to the truth, despite lacking probabilistic justification)."],"premise":"The target claims that accepting the pragmatic defense forces Popper to concede that corroboration offers no grip on verisimilitude, thereby reducing science to a game of survival without teleological direction.","successor_question":"If the pragmatic preference for better-corroborated theories is the only rational way to pursue verisimilitude without induction, does the 'cost' identified by the target actually exist, or is it merely a rejection of inductive certainty rather than teleological direction?","target":"cfcd00ba54028b01557b31ed255c30ecdd1cd89c79afdfef35ddc69bfc398732"}]}